[38;5;231m⏺[39m I'm classifying this as [1mbounded[0m: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m already exists in [38;5;153mapp.js:4[39m, and this is a signature change in one file. So
  I'll present a short design in chat rather than write a spec. One thing I need to settle first.

  [1mThe fork:[0m nothing in this repo produces a [38;5;153muserId[39m. The form collects only username and password ([38;5;153mindex.html:9-10[39m), and
  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is called with exactly those ([38;5;153mapp.js:23[39m). So "add a userId parameter" needs a source for that value, and the
  three plausible sources lead to different interfaces:

  - [1mCaller passes it in[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m. The submit handler must get a userId from somewhere new (a
    session/device/tenant id). Cheap to write, but it pushes the real question one level up: you'd still need to decide
    what that id is and where it's stored. Rules out nothing later.
  - [1muserId comes back from login, not into it[0m — the server assigns it and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m. No
    new parameter at all. This is the usual shape for "track who logged in," since the client doesn't know the id before
    authenticating. Changes the return contract instead of the signature — also cheap now, harder to reverse once
    callers read the field.
  - [1mNew form field[0m — add a userId input the person types. Simplest to wire, but users don't normally know their own id,
    and it makes the field part of the UI contract, which is the most expensive of the three to undo.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the second. [38;5;153musername[39m already identifies who logged in for logging purposes, and a userId that the
  client has to supply [3mbefore[0m authentication is usually a sign the value really belongs in the response. But if you have
  an external id already in hand (a tenant or analytics id), the first is right and I'll do that.
